course: dictionary
lesson: 13004
title: Outside, inside and the exterior
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exterior means the outside of something. Its opposite is interior, the inside. «The exterior of the school was painted bright yellow.» Here exterior is a noun: it is the outside part of the building.
explain: Exterior can also be an adjective, describing something that is on the outside. «The exterior walls were made of red brick.» These are the outer walls, the ones you can see from the street. To check, swap in outer: the outer walls were made of red brick still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes exterior means how someone seems on the outside, which can be different from how they feel inside. «Under his cool exterior, Sam was very nervous about the test.» Sam looked calm, but inside he felt worried. The word under is the clue that something is hidden.
fact: Exterior means the outside of something.
fact: Interior means the inside, so it is the opposite of exterior.
fact: As an adjective, exterior means on the outside or outer, as in exterior paint.
fact: «Exterior» can describe how a person looks on the outside, and that may hide how they really feel.
fact: Clue words such as «under», «but» and «hid» often show that the word means something different from what you can see.
exercise: «The exterior of the box was covered in silver paper.» What is the exterior of the box? | The string tied around it | The inside of the box | The lid only | *The outside of the box
exercise: Which word means the opposite of exterior? | exterior | outer | *interior | roof
exercise: «Under his calm exterior, Ben was scared.» What does exterior mean here? | The inside of his bag | A paint for the outside of walls | The outer wall of a house | *How Ben looked on the outside, not how he felt
test:
«The exterior walls of the shed needed fresh paint.» What does exterior describe? | *The outside walls | The inside shelves | The garden gate | The floor
test:
«She hid her sadness behind a happy exterior.» Why might she have a happy exterior? | *To look happy on the outside while she felt sad inside | Because she was painting the house | To show her real feelings to everyone | Because it was her birthday
exam:
«The exterior of the new bus was red, but its interior was grey and comfy.» What does interior mean here? | *The inside | The outside | The front seat | The wheels
